00:07:08.352 --> 00:07:09.310
a couple of months ago.

00:07:09.310 --> 00:07:11.860
And coincidentally,
today, we're wrapping up

00:07:11.860 --> 00:07:14.086
the world tour in Chicago.

00:07:14.086 --> 00:07:15.460
But on our technical
track, we're

00:07:15.460 --> 00:07:18.940
always talking about the
aspects of an API program

00:07:18.940 --> 00:07:20.550
that make us successful.

00:07:20.550 --> 00:07:23.150
And when you look
at the actual APIs,

00:07:23.150 --> 00:07:25.420
a lot of the things that
make them successful--

00:07:25.420 --> 00:07:27.520
but like I mentioned
before, making them easily

00:07:27.520 --> 00:07:29.770
consumable by
developers-- are things

00:07:29.770 --> 00:07:32.200
like having a standard,
consistent interface.

00:07:32.200 --> 00:07:34.270
And you get that by
applying that facade

00:07:34.270 --> 00:07:37.360
and not leaving the interface
up to each individual service

00:07:37.360 --> 00:07:38.289
developer.

00:07:38.289 --> 00:07:40.330
The fact that there's a
consistent resource model

00:07:40.330 --> 00:07:41.440
to code against--

00:07:41.440 --> 00:07:44.500
all these things that
make APIs easy to use also

00:07:44.500 --> 00:07:46.780
make them very easy
to use by attackers

00:07:46.780 --> 00:07:48.490
who want to exploit
vulnerabilities

00:07:48.490 --> 00:07:52.080
in your back ends.

00:07:52.080 --> 00:07:55.140
So what kind of
tools do you have

00:07:55.140 --> 00:07:56.730
if you're leveraging
Apigee in order

00:07:56.730 --> 00:07:59.620
to combat some of these attacks?

00:07:59.620 --> 00:08:04.110
So this picture shows
an end-to-end view

00:08:04.110 --> 00:08:06.540
of the kinds of features
we have in place to help

00:08:06.540 --> 00:08:08.760
protect your APIs.

00:08:08.760 --> 00:08:13.920
Starting at the client, we have
the ability to leverage TLS--

00:08:13.920 --> 00:08:19.290
of course, one-way or two-way
TLS-- between the client

00:08:19.290 --> 00:08:21.390
apps and Apigee itself.

00:08:21.390 --> 00:08:25.230
We have OAuth policies to
enable OAuth protections,

00:08:25.230 --> 00:08:29.349
API key policies, JWT
policies all out of the box.

00:08:29.349 --> 00:08:31.890
I'm not going to get into all
this and read this whole slide.

00:08:31.890 --> 00:08:32.973
There's a lot of features.

00:08:32.973 --> 00:08:36.090
But at the proxy
level, similarly, we

00:08:36.090 --> 00:08:38.340
have access control, OAuth.

00:08:38.340 --> 00:08:43.080
We protect the back end as
well with TLS, IP whitelisting.

00:08:43.080 --> 00:08:46.020
And then the platform itself
has full role-based access

00:08:46.020 --> 00:08:49.740
control, both in the platform
for your organization's users,

00:08:49.740 --> 00:08:51.640
as well as in the
developer portal.

00:08:51.640 --> 00:08:55.560
So when a consumer comes, they
see the right API products,

00:08:55.560 --> 00:08:57.840
the right APIs, the
right level of access

00:08:57.840 --> 00:08:59.316
that you want them to see.

00:08:59.316 --> 00:09:00.690
And you may have
a different view

00:09:00.690 --> 00:09:05.280
for the developers internal
to your organization

00:09:05.280 --> 00:09:07.350
versus external third parties.

00:09:07.350 --> 00:09:11.340
So all of that end to end is
all done through configuration.

00:09:11.340 --> 00:09:12.400
There is no code.

00:09:12.400 --> 00:09:16.830
This is configuring
features or drag and drop

00:09:16.830 --> 00:09:20.002
with policies to do
all this enforcement.

00:09:22.720 --> 00:09:26.200
So there are lots of attacks,
and we hear about these

00:09:26.200 --> 00:09:27.530
all the time.

00:09:27.530 --> 00:09:30.580
There was a pretty bad breach
just about two weeks ago

00:09:30.580 --> 00:09:35.170
where a major social
network ended up

00:09:35.170 --> 00:09:37.270
allowing the
harvesting of hundreds

00:09:37.270 --> 00:09:39.350
of millions of user accounts.

00:09:39.350 --> 00:09:44.410
So that's an attack that falls
into one of the major two

00:09:44.410 --> 00:09:46.120
categories we see.

00:09:46.120 --> 00:09:47.860
And there are more
categories in this,

00:09:47.860 --> 00:09:50.650
but the vast majority
of them go into account

00:09:50.650 --> 00:09:54.280
takeover-type attacks
and scraping attacks.

00:09:54.280 --> 00:09:57.980
So the one I was just
discussing is a scraping attack

00:09:57.980 --> 00:10:00.770
where a vulnerability
which allowed access

00:10:00.770 --> 00:10:04.460
to more resources than was
intended by the platform

00:10:04.460 --> 00:10:10.790
allowed a malicious application
to scrape far more data out

00:10:10.790 --> 00:10:13.830
and then retain that data and
use it for its own purposes.

00:10:13.830 --> 00:10:16.790
So all these attacks
are things that you

00:10:16.790 --> 00:10:18.040
need to be concerned with.

00:10:18.040 --> 00:10:20.210
And they're all
things that we're

00:10:20.210 --> 00:10:23.360
seeing across a broad
range of our customers.

00:10:23.360 --> 00:10:26.180
Very few of our customers that
we've evaluated and worked with

00:10:26.180 --> 00:10:29.997
have not seen a large number
of these attacks occurring live

00:10:29.997 --> 00:10:30.580
on their APIs.

00:10:33.410 --> 00:10:37.409
So this is a quick graphic
here to demonstrate

00:10:37.409 --> 00:10:39.200
a couple of the different
kinds of attacks.

00:10:39.200 --> 00:10:43.500
And first, we're all familiar
with denial of service attacks.

00:10:43.500 --> 00:10:46.310
And as a GCP customer,
you're already

00:10:46.310 --> 00:10:51.860
leveraging a massive, massive
data center bisection bandwidth

00:10:51.860 --> 00:10:54.470
in excess of 1,000
terabits per second.

00:10:54.470 --> 00:10:57.530
So from a DoS
perspective, you're

00:10:57.530 --> 00:10:59.900
being protected by a
cloud provider that's

00:10:59.900 --> 00:11:03.920
able to absorb an immense
amount of traffic.

00:11:03.920 --> 00:11:05.540
But there are other
kinds of attacks.

00:11:05.540 --> 00:11:08.480
So if you look at here,
we've got the ability

00:11:08.480 --> 00:11:12.350
to do volume-based
and region or blocking

00:11:12.350 --> 00:11:15.040
based on known bad ISPs or IPs.

00:11:15.040 --> 00:11:18.140
And that will block
your DoS attacks.

00:11:18.140 --> 00:11:19.950
But a lot of things
will still get through

00:11:19.950 --> 00:11:23.810
because these malicious
bot creators know

00:11:23.810 --> 00:11:25.850
how to stay under the radar.

00:11:25.850 --> 00:11:28.790
They know that if they send
millions of requests a minute,

00:11:28.790 --> 00:11:30.020
this will be blocked.

00:11:30.020 --> 00:11:32.600
They also know potentially
that you have--

00:11:32.600 --> 00:11:35.000
because you have a developer
portal that says so--

00:11:35.000 --> 00:11:37.640
that you allow 500
requests an hour.

00:11:37.640 --> 00:11:41.870
So we see bot writers
creating bots that come

00:11:41.870 --> 00:11:45.710
in at a randomized
number of requests-- say,

00:11:45.710 --> 00:11:48.450
between 4 and 450 requests--

00:11:48.450 --> 00:11:51.590
so it looks and blends in
with your normal traffic.

00:11:51.590 --> 00:11:54.410
So it's important that
we have a different way

00:11:54.410 --> 00:11:57.080
to address these
types of attacks

00:11:57.080 --> 00:11:59.880
that look like human traffic.

00:11:59.880 --> 00:12:04.080
So it ends up that
the security model

00:12:04.080 --> 00:12:08.940
is very different with APIs than
it is with traditional systems

00:12:08.940 --> 00:12:12.750
before we started exposing
our APIs in such a wide way

00:12:12.750 --> 00:12:14.370
with external entities.

00:12:14.370 --> 00:12:17.070
So you need protection
that's purpose-built.

00:12:17.070 --> 00:12:20.010
And it's going to separate
the bad from the good traffic,

00:12:20.010 --> 00:12:22.020
both from your good clients--

00:12:22.020 --> 00:12:26.370
because for a known
partner of yours,

00:12:26.370 --> 00:12:28.920
they'll still make bad calls.

00:12:28.920 --> 00:12:30.450
For instance,
exceeding a quota--

00:12:30.450 --> 00:12:31.920
we need to block that.

00:12:31.920 --> 00:12:37.380
For instance, issuing a request
with an expired API key--

00:12:37.380 --> 00:12:38.650
you need to block that.

00:12:38.650 --> 00:12:41.190
But then there are all
these bad actors as well.

00:12:41.190 --> 00:12:44.070
And this is very difficult
with those types of calls

00:12:44.070 --> 00:12:45.090
to distinguish.

00:12:45.090 --> 00:12:47.675
And that's where we
introduced Apigee Sense.

00:12:47.675 --> 00:12:49.050
And this was in
response to a lot

00:12:49.050 --> 00:12:53.460
of our customers experiencing
high volumes of bot traffic.

00:12:53.460 --> 00:12:55.860
And they came to us and
said, this is difficult

00:12:55.860 --> 00:12:57.831
because we have set up quotas.

00:12:57.831 --> 00:12:59.580
We've established all
of the right things.

00:12:59.580 --> 00:13:02.460
But those requests
are still coming in.

00:13:02.460 --> 00:13:06.480
And although they don't
always penetrate the system,

00:13:06.480 --> 00:13:09.360
we can see through our
analytics that they're there.

00:13:09.360 --> 00:13:10.560
Those requests are there.

00:13:10.560 --> 00:13:14.770
And they wanted us to
help them address this.

00:13:14.770 --> 00:13:16.870
So how does Sense work?

00:13:16.870 --> 00:13:21.400
So as I mentioned, it's
built specifically for APIs.

00:13:21.400 --> 00:13:24.550
And we've leveraged
the fact that we

00:13:24.550 --> 00:13:26.620
process trillions
and trillions of API

00:13:26.620 --> 00:13:33.710
calls to learn what are the
bad behavioral patterns.

00:13:33.710 --> 00:13:37.490
And this is, again,
not a deterministic,

00:13:37.490 --> 00:13:40.230
this one particular call is
exhibiting this behavior.

00:13:40.230 --> 00:13:47.470
It's more about over time, this
particular IP, particular ISP,

00:13:47.470 --> 00:13:50.830
set of IPs is exhibiting
this behavior.

00:13:50.830 --> 00:13:53.890
And that is something
that we can detect.

00:13:53.890 --> 00:13:55.270
And it happens in a closed loop.

00:13:55.270 --> 00:13:57.810
And it gets smarter and
smarter as you use it

00:13:57.810 --> 00:14:03.230
and as you feed rules
into the system.

00:14:03.230 --> 00:14:06.630
So Sense runs in the background.

00:14:06.630 --> 00:14:10.130
And it leverages
the analytics data

00:14:10.130 --> 00:14:12.410
that we're already collecting,
so there's very little

00:14:12.410 --> 00:14:13.970
overhead.

00:14:13.970 --> 00:14:16.580
We've built the
models for detection

00:14:16.580 --> 00:14:19.640
based on our understanding
of all of our customers

00:14:19.640 --> 00:14:21.000
on our cloud.

00:14:21.000 --> 00:14:24.410
However, your data as an
individual Apigee customer

00:14:24.410 --> 00:14:27.020
is never commingled with
anybody else's data,

00:14:27.020 --> 00:14:28.550
any other customer's data.

00:14:28.550 --> 00:14:31.640
Our tenancy model
disallows that.

00:14:31.640 --> 00:14:34.520
But we have leveraged the fact
that we're the largest API

00:14:34.520 --> 00:14:38.330
management platform to
gain insights about what

00:14:38.330 --> 00:14:41.870
kinds of patterns matter and
how to protect against them.

00:14:41.870 --> 00:14:45.740
So of note also is the
patterns that I mentioned.

00:14:45.740 --> 00:14:48.290
And we'll look at them
in a minute in the demo.

00:14:48.290 --> 00:14:51.530
We've established the
patterns, but they're

00:14:51.530 --> 00:14:53.330
completely tunable by you.

00:14:53.330 --> 00:14:55.724
So you start out with
sensible defaults,

00:14:55.724 --> 00:14:57.140
and then you can
tune them by you.

00:14:57.140 --> 00:14:59.431
And you can even introduce
your own rules and patterns.

00:15:02.260 --> 00:15:06.170
So Sense operates in
two high-level phases.

00:15:06.170 --> 00:15:08.589
There's detection
and protection.

00:15:08.589 --> 00:15:10.380
In detection, as I
mentioned, the traffic's

00:15:10.380 --> 00:15:12.090
analyzed in real time.

00:15:12.090 --> 00:15:15.660
And the alerts when we
detect suspicious behavior--

00:15:15.660 --> 00:15:17.670
and I say "suspicious."

00:15:17.670 --> 00:15:19.620
We don't know when
we detect something

00:15:19.620 --> 00:15:20.860
that it is bad traffic.

00:15:20.860 --> 00:15:22.770
But we know that it
matches a pattern that we

00:15:22.770 --> 00:15:24.750
suspect might be bad traffic.

00:15:24.750 --> 00:15:28.994
So we issue alerts immediately
to your administrators.

00:15:28.994 --> 00:15:29.910
And then they come in.

00:15:29.910 --> 00:15:32.100
And they get a full
detailed report

00:15:32.100 --> 00:15:34.980
that shows the types of
attacks that are coming in

00:15:34.980 --> 00:15:37.880
and the behaviors
they're exhibiting.

00:15:37.880 --> 00:15:40.800
You can dig into the logs
and analyze exactly what

00:15:40.800 --> 00:15:42.420
the requests look like.

00:15:42.420 --> 00:15:45.000
And that gives your
operational administrators

00:15:45.000 --> 00:15:47.340
and your security
admins the ability

00:15:47.340 --> 00:15:49.950
to detect or to start
thinking about how

00:15:49.950 --> 00:15:52.210
to act on that traffic.

00:15:52.210 --> 00:15:53.880
So that's phase one.

00:15:53.880 --> 00:15:56.310
And I mentioned we'll
look at some of the rules.

00:15:56.310 --> 00:16:00.750
But we have rules about
unusual error rates,

00:16:00.750 --> 00:16:04.770
accessing a larger than expected
surface area of your APIs.

00:16:04.770 --> 00:16:08.280
If you have a catalog, for
instance, in the retail space,

00:16:08.280 --> 00:16:11.340
it's atypical for a
customer to access 60%

00:16:11.340 --> 00:16:12.810
of the items in your catalog.

00:16:12.810 --> 00:16:15.750
That would be a trigger for
a bad behavioral pattern.

00:16:19.050 --> 00:16:21.030
So the next step is protection.

00:16:21.030 --> 00:16:23.070
So now we've given
you this dashboard.

00:16:23.070 --> 00:16:24.840
And we've given
you alerts to let

00:16:24.840 --> 00:16:29.980
you know that we have suspicious
activity potentially happening.

00:16:29.980 --> 00:16:32.790
So you'll see a dashboard
where you can then

00:16:32.790 --> 00:16:35.010
act on all of these requests.

00:16:35.010 --> 00:16:36.330
And you can choose--

00:16:36.330 --> 00:16:38.410
and this is a big
differentiator here--

00:16:38.410 --> 00:16:41.190
is you can choose to
allow that traffic

00:16:41.190 --> 00:16:43.110
to proceed into the system.

00:16:43.110 --> 00:16:44.970
You can choose to block it.

00:16:44.970 --> 00:16:47.350
Or you can choose to flag it.

00:16:47.350 --> 00:16:50.160
And this gives you a
really flexible way

00:16:50.160 --> 00:16:52.870
of handling the traffic.

00:16:52.870 --> 00:16:54.870
And we'll dive in a little
more into what I mean

00:16:54.870 --> 00:16:58.140
by that flexible protection.

00:16:58.140 --> 00:17:02.950
But last, I want just drive
home this closed loop.

00:17:02.950 --> 00:17:06.030
So the closed-loop system
that we've implemented

00:17:06.030 --> 00:17:07.240
does a couple of things.

00:17:07.240 --> 00:17:10.650
One is because we're leveraging
analytics data that we already

00:17:10.650 --> 00:17:14.040
capture anyway, we
add very little,

00:17:14.040 --> 00:17:16.319
almost no latency to requests.

00:17:16.319 --> 00:17:18.869
In fact, we had no
latency at all to detect.

00:17:18.869 --> 00:17:21.630
We had less than 10
milliseconds of latency

00:17:21.630 --> 00:17:23.190
when we are protecting.

00:17:23.190 --> 00:17:26.119
And that's a matter of if
you want to block or allow

00:17:26.119 --> 00:17:27.329
or flag traffic.

00:17:27.329 --> 00:17:29.550
So it's a super-low
latency solution

00:17:29.550 --> 00:17:32.740
because it runs in a closed
loop offline with data

00:17:32.740 --> 00:17:33.967
we're already collecting.

00:17:36.890 --> 00:17:38.690
And a little more on
flexible protection--

00:17:38.690 --> 00:17:40.610
there's two things
we mean when we

00:17:40.610 --> 00:17:42.410
talk about flexible protection.

00:17:42.410 --> 00:17:44.480
One is what you can do.

00:17:44.480 --> 00:17:46.910
What kind of actions
can you take?

00:17:46.910 --> 00:17:50.900
I mentioned that you can block
traffic, you can allow traffic,

00:17:50.900 --> 00:17:53.250
or you can flag traffic.

00:17:53.250 --> 00:17:55.190
So what is flagging?

00:17:55.190 --> 00:17:58.820
What that means is adding
a header, for instance,

00:17:58.820 --> 00:18:01.549
to a request to
say, maybe I don't

00:18:01.549 --> 00:18:02.840
want to block this immediately.

00:18:02.840 --> 00:18:04.390
I'm aware that this is a bot.

00:18:04.390 --> 00:18:06.860
I'm aware they're
scraping my data.

00:18:06.860 --> 00:18:09.470
But instead of blocking them
so they can go and retool

00:18:09.470 --> 00:18:11.870
their bot and hit
me again, I might

00:18:11.870 --> 00:18:14.090
want to send them
a delayed response.

00:18:14.090 --> 00:18:16.102
I might want to
manipulate the data.

00:18:16.102 --> 00:18:17.810
I can manipulate the
data in the request.

00:18:17.810 --> 00:18:20.730
I can send them
static pricing data.

00:18:20.730 --> 00:18:22.040
I can dummy the data up.

00:18:22.040 --> 00:18:23.660
I can remove fields from it.

00:18:23.660 --> 00:18:26.190
There's all kinds of things
I can do to that data.

00:18:26.190 --> 00:18:28.820
And that's a really
powerful tool

00:18:28.820 --> 00:18:32.340
because the bot developers are
looking at their responses.

00:18:32.340 --> 00:18:35.690
So sometimes, blocking
is the right choice.

00:18:35.690 --> 00:18:39.320
Sometimes, you want to
do something specific

00:18:39.320 --> 00:18:41.300
to handle that in a
different kind of way.

00:18:41.300 --> 00:18:43.880
And allowing is another
important aspect

00:18:43.880 --> 00:18:50.980
because we allow our partners
to work against our APIs

00:18:50.980 --> 00:18:52.930
in a bot-like way.

00:18:52.930 --> 00:18:55.450
I have many customers
who have partners

00:18:55.450 --> 00:18:59.080
that write bots to pull
down data on a daily basis.

00:18:59.080 --> 00:19:02.830
So that would appear to
Apigee Sense as a bot.

00:19:02.830 --> 00:19:06.790
But your security admins can go
and allow all of that traffic

00:19:06.790 --> 00:19:09.810
for its particular
duration or perpetuity

00:19:09.810 --> 00:19:12.010
so that that partner is not--

00:19:12.010 --> 00:19:13.900
its traffic is not blocked.

00:19:13.900 --> 00:19:16.660
So the other aspect
of flexible protection

00:19:16.660 --> 00:19:19.600
is configuration or code.

00:19:19.600 --> 00:19:24.050
So the first that I mentioned
is how you act on your traffic.

00:19:24.050 --> 00:19:26.650
And then the next
part is specifically

00:19:26.650 --> 00:19:29.680
about the case where
you flag traffic.

00:19:29.680 --> 00:19:32.470
As I mentioned, we're going to
enrich a request with a header.

00:19:32.470 --> 00:19:35.710
That means once that request
gets into the Apigee processing

00:19:35.710 --> 00:19:39.130
runtime, you have the ability
to do anything you like to it.

00:19:39.130 --> 00:19:42.790
That means you can leverage
one of the nearly 40 policies

00:19:42.790 --> 00:19:45.310
in Apigee to do
things like traffic

00:19:45.310 --> 00:19:46.930
shaping, conditional routing.

00:19:46.930 --> 00:19:50.320
And all of these things are
done simply by configuration.

00:19:50.320 --> 00:19:54.400
It's a policy that you
drag into your API flow,

00:19:54.400 --> 00:19:57.550
you tune the attributes,
and it works.

00:19:57.550 --> 00:20:00.670
The other choice of how you
deal with flagged traffic

00:20:00.670 --> 00:20:01.870
is through code.

00:20:01.870 --> 00:20:04.120
And we have extension
policies that

00:20:04.120 --> 00:20:09.850
allow you to write JavaScript,
Python, Java, or Node.js

00:20:09.850 --> 00:20:15.010
if you want to extend the
capabilities for handling

00:20:15.010 --> 00:20:16.410
that flagged traffic.

00:20:16.410 --> 00:20:19.480
So it gives you a lot of
different flexible options

00:20:19.480 --> 00:20:22.030
on how to deal with
these potential attacks.

00:20:24.909 --> 00:20:25.950
So I have a graphic here.

00:20:25.950 --> 00:20:28.260
And I want to talk
a little bit--

00:20:28.260 --> 00:20:31.650
the way I think
about API security

00:20:31.650 --> 00:20:34.680
is it's about layers of defense.

00:20:34.680 --> 00:20:39.910
And the best analogy that comes
to mind for me is this here.

00:20:39.910 --> 00:20:41.260
If you look at this castle--

00:20:41.260 --> 00:20:44.200
and imagine for a moment
that the walls here

00:20:44.200 --> 00:20:46.150
are your API policies.

00:20:46.150 --> 00:20:52.140
Your walls are defining that
OAuth 2.0 is required to enter.

00:20:52.140 --> 00:20:54.100
So they're going to
block those requests.

00:20:54.100 --> 00:20:55.840
You've got some
throttling going on

00:20:55.840 --> 00:20:57.940
because you have
this one defined

00:20:57.940 --> 00:21:03.310
entry point with a solid door,
maybe guards in the gatehouse.

00:21:03.310 --> 00:21:06.590
And it looks pretty secure,
difficult to penetrate.

00:21:06.590 --> 00:21:08.690
But the problem--
there's a problem here.

00:21:08.690 --> 00:21:12.220
The problem is all around
the castle are trees,

00:21:12.220 --> 00:21:14.210
and there's limited visibility.

00:21:14.210 --> 00:21:19.300
So at any moment, there
could be many attackers

00:21:19.300 --> 00:21:22.082
under the tree line chipping
away at that brick wall.

00:21:22.082 --> 00:21:23.540
And they may never
make it through.

00:21:23.540 --> 00:21:24.915
But if you give
them enough time,

00:21:24.915 --> 00:21:26.230
the odds are that they will.

00:21:26.230 --> 00:21:29.500
So just because you've
got strong walls

00:21:29.500 --> 00:21:32.500
and a defined entry point
doesn't completely protect you.

00:21:32.500 --> 00:21:35.920
You need visibility.

00:21:35.920 --> 00:21:38.310
So here's a different view.

00:21:38.310 --> 00:21:41.436
It's a similar castle.

00:21:41.436 --> 00:21:43.530
There's a throttling
mechanism that

00:21:43.530 --> 00:21:46.650
brings traffic into one entry
point with a strong gate.

00:21:46.650 --> 00:21:49.650
You've got the same walls with
the same OAuth protection,

00:21:49.650 --> 00:21:51.810
but they have a moat.

00:21:51.810 --> 00:21:53.460
And they've cleared
out all the trees.

00:21:53.460 --> 00:21:57.870
So this API provider
can see for miles

00:21:57.870 --> 00:22:00.940
as people are attempting
to come in and attack.

00:22:00.940 --> 00:22:03.060
And that gives him the
ability to figure out

00:22:03.060 --> 00:22:04.560
how to respond to them.

00:22:04.560 --> 00:22:06.930
If it's a friendly
party, no worries.

00:22:06.930 --> 00:22:09.159
You might want to
block that party.

00:22:09.159 --> 00:22:10.950
So that's kind of the
way I think about it.

00:22:10.950 --> 00:22:13.050
It's layers and
layers of security.

00:22:13.050 --> 00:22:17.940
And the Sense feature that
we're talking about a few slides

00:22:17.940 --> 00:22:22.620
back is an additional layer
beyond the DoS protection

00:22:22.620 --> 00:22:26.010
you get in GCP, beyond
the full suite of security

00:22:26.010 --> 00:22:29.970
policies already
offered by Apigee Edge

00:22:29.970 --> 00:22:33.690
to handle that traffic that
looks like human traffic

00:22:33.690 --> 00:22:39.450
or looks like friendly
traffic but is not.

00:22:39.450 --> 00:22:42.600
And then, of course, protection.

00:22:42.600 --> 00:22:46.830
We mentioned detect-- the
visibility helps us with that--

00:22:46.830 --> 00:22:48.100
and protect.

00:22:48.100 --> 00:22:50.557
So as unfriendlies
are approaching,

00:22:50.557 --> 00:22:52.140
we know exactly how
to deal with them.

00:22:54.544 --> 00:22:55.960
So at this time,
I'm going to jump

00:22:55.960 --> 00:22:58.790
into a quick demo
of Apigee Sense

00:22:58.790 --> 00:23:01.080
to show you what the
dashboards look like

00:23:01.080 --> 00:23:02.621
and how you might
interact with them.

00:23:12.241 --> 00:23:14.380
All right.

00:23:14.380 --> 00:23:15.320
OK.

00:23:15.320 --> 00:23:19.250
So what you can see right
here is the Sense dashboard.

00:23:19.250 --> 00:23:24.290
And right now, we're on
the bot analysis report.

00:23:24.290 --> 00:23:28.470
Let me switch into the rules and
show you what those looks like.

00:23:28.470 --> 00:23:30.860
So I mentioned that we
have a lot of rules baked

00:23:30.860 --> 00:23:35.960
into Sense that are designed
and actually were built based

00:23:35.960 --> 00:23:39.920
on the trillions of API
calls that we receive

00:23:39.920 --> 00:23:43.740
and our deep experience working
with all of our customers.

00:23:43.740 --> 00:23:46.490
So we have things
like content quota

00:23:46.490 --> 00:23:48.350
exceeder, OAuth collector.

00:23:48.350 --> 00:23:49.330
I mentioned scraping.

00:23:49.330 --> 00:23:51.110
We have content scrapers.

00:23:51.110 --> 00:23:53.510
We have guessers.

00:23:53.510 --> 00:23:57.340
Each one of these is combating
a particular type of bot

00:23:57.340 --> 00:23:59.450
that generally fall
into a few categories.

00:23:59.450 --> 00:24:03.020
But let's look at
a couple of these.

00:24:03.020 --> 00:24:06.090
So OAuth collector-- and
you have a description here.

00:24:06.090 --> 00:24:08.350
It's a high number
of OAuth sessions

00:24:08.350 --> 00:24:11.430
but a small number of
user-agent families.

00:24:11.430 --> 00:24:15.590
So here, you can define the
ranges of what's acceptable

00:24:15.590 --> 00:24:17.590
and what's expected behavior.

00:24:17.590 --> 00:24:20.340
Now, I can define these
ranges for that rule.

00:24:20.340 --> 00:24:25.040
And that rule will simply
control with those new defined

00:24:25.040 --> 00:24:28.690
parameters when suspicious
activity is highlighted

00:24:28.690 --> 00:24:30.900
or when activity would be
highlighted as suspicious.

00:24:30.900 --> 00:24:32.240
And I'd be alerted.

00:24:32.240 --> 00:24:37.322
And the policies are all
similarly configured.

00:24:37.322 --> 00:24:39.530
They have different levels
of attributes for each one

00:24:39.530 --> 00:24:44.240
and different minimums and
maximums in their ranges.

00:24:44.240 --> 00:24:47.600
But they all effectively
follow this pattern

00:24:47.600 --> 00:24:50.840
where it's an established
set of attributes,

00:24:50.840 --> 00:24:55.950
and you configure the range that
defines suspicious behavior.

00:24:55.950 --> 00:24:58.550
You also have the ability
to create your own rules.

00:24:58.550 --> 00:25:02.960
If there are specific attacks
that are relative to you

00:25:02.960 --> 00:25:04.430
or you feel you
want to investigate

00:25:04.430 --> 00:25:07.080
a particular area of concern,
you can build your own rules

00:25:07.080 --> 00:25:07.580
as well.

00:25:10.280 --> 00:25:12.330
The next-- I can
look at the report.

00:25:12.330 --> 00:25:15.210
The detection report--
as I mentioned, again,

00:25:15.210 --> 00:25:19.599
just to remind the audience,
it's about detect and protect.

00:25:19.599 --> 00:25:22.140
And the first section, which is
where we still are right now,

00:25:22.140 --> 00:25:23.370
is about detection.

00:25:23.370 --> 00:25:27.465
I want to look at a report of
what's going on in my platform.

00:25:34.120 --> 00:25:36.240
Little issue here.

00:25:36.240 --> 00:25:45.990
Just let me refresh the page,
get in the right organization.

00:25:45.990 --> 00:25:46.490
All right.

00:25:46.490 --> 00:25:47.420
Here we go.

00:25:47.420 --> 00:25:49.780
So over here, I can
see the total traffic

00:25:49.780 --> 00:25:52.380
that's coming into my system
over this particular time

00:25:52.380 --> 00:25:52.880
range.

00:25:52.880 --> 00:25:54.764
And I can adjust my time range.

00:25:54.764 --> 00:25:55.930
And I'm showing all of them.

00:25:55.930 --> 00:25:59.470
I can filter by the
requests that are already

00:25:59.470 --> 00:26:02.080
defined to be allowed,
blocked, or flagged,

00:26:02.080 --> 00:26:04.730
or ones that I've
not taken action yet.

00:26:04.730 --> 00:26:08.530
So at this point, I can
see the kinds of behaviors

00:26:08.530 --> 00:26:09.870
that Sense is detecting.

00:26:09.870 --> 00:26:12.880
It's detecting one guesser
bot, a login guesser,

00:26:12.880 --> 00:26:15.730
an OAuth abuser, and
an OAuth collector.

00:26:15.730 --> 00:26:17.850
And this is the traffic
from each of those.

00:26:17.850 --> 00:26:23.190
I can directly act here, which
allows me to build a protection

00:26:23.190 --> 00:26:26.770
rule to enforce a particular
action on these types

00:26:26.770 --> 00:26:28.890
of requests in the future.

00:26:28.890 --> 00:26:33.870
I can dig into the
details of them if I like.

00:26:33.870 --> 00:26:35.640
I can even look at
a list view, which

00:26:35.640 --> 00:26:38.930
shows particular requests.

00:26:38.930 --> 00:26:43.110
This is a particular IP address,
its activity over the time

00:26:43.110 --> 00:26:46.380
range that we've
set, and the types

00:26:46.380 --> 00:26:52.040
of rules that it's triggering.

00:26:52.040 --> 00:26:53.940
And here, I can also
act on it, which

00:26:53.940 --> 00:26:56.890
will create a rule for me.

00:26:56.890 --> 00:26:59.410
So that's the detection side.

00:26:59.410 --> 00:27:01.482
Now, if I look at protection--

00:27:01.482 --> 00:27:03.190
again, let's look at
the protection rules

00:27:03.190 --> 00:27:05.211
that are already established.

00:27:05.211 --> 00:27:06.710
And these are ones
that have already

00:27:06.710 --> 00:27:09.500
been set based on
suspicious activity

00:27:09.500 --> 00:27:11.870
that Sense has
highlighted to me.

00:27:11.870 --> 00:27:13.250
So these rules
have been created.

00:27:13.250 --> 00:27:14.660
I can add rules.

00:27:14.660 --> 00:27:16.470
I can filter them.

00:27:16.470 --> 00:27:18.420
And you can see
exactly the definition

00:27:18.420 --> 00:27:20.250
and how long they are lasting.

00:27:20.250 --> 00:27:23.980
This is a short-lived rule
that will last for a few hours.

00:27:23.980 --> 00:27:25.890
This one will always
be there because it's

00:27:25.890 --> 00:27:26.910
my trusted partner.

00:27:26.910 --> 00:27:29.130
I don't expect them to change.

00:27:29.130 --> 00:27:31.200
If they change,
I'll work with Sense

00:27:31.200 --> 00:27:33.010
to configure the rule for them.

00:27:33.010 --> 00:27:35.810
But I want my trusted partner
to always come through

00:27:35.810 --> 00:27:36.810
with their friendly bot.

00:27:39.380 --> 00:27:43.460
But I can create a rule,
and I can block traffic.

00:27:43.460 --> 00:27:46.520
I can have it expire
whenever I like.

00:27:46.520 --> 00:27:48.980
And I can block by bot reason.

00:27:48.980 --> 00:27:55.080
Let's block all brute
guessers and have

00:27:55.080 --> 00:27:57.700
that expire in 12 hours.

00:27:57.700 --> 00:27:58.635
And yes, it's active.

00:28:03.020 --> 00:28:07.335
Sorry-- block brute guessers.

00:28:11.030 --> 00:28:12.350
So I've created that rule.

00:28:12.350 --> 00:28:15.050
And from this point
forward, Sense

00:28:15.050 --> 00:28:19.160
will automatically and instantly
enforce this blocking rule.

00:28:19.160 --> 00:28:22.430
Now, had I made a flagging
rule, what Sense would do

00:28:22.430 --> 00:28:24.410
is add that header
that I can then

00:28:24.410 --> 00:28:27.482
go modify my Apigee
API to respond

00:28:27.482 --> 00:28:28.940
to that header in
a particular way.

00:28:31.660 --> 00:28:33.880
So that's a quick run through.

00:28:33.880 --> 00:28:37.750
There's a lot more I could
show you of Apigee Sense

00:28:37.750 --> 00:28:40.370
and, of course, of the
entire Apigee platform.

00:28:40.370 --> 00:28:43.090
But in the time
we have, hopefully

00:28:43.090 --> 00:28:49.930
I covered the end-to-end flow
of how Apigee Sense works.

00:28:49.930 --> 00:28:53.520
So I'm going to hop back here.

00:29:05.320 --> 00:29:05.820
All right.

00:29:05.820 --> 00:29:06.960
Sorry about that delay.

00:29:06.960 --> 00:29:10.500
So hopefully, this
session was informative.

00:29:10.500 --> 00:29:12.930
Hopefully, you
learned a few things.

00:29:12.930 --> 00:29:15.940
If you want to learn more,
there are lots of ways to do so.

00:29:15.940 --> 00:29:18.150
So first and foremost,
Apigee offers

00:29:18.150 --> 00:29:22.230
a free trial where you get
unlimited use of the platform.

00:29:22.230 --> 00:29:25.060
And you can spin
up an organization,

00:29:25.060 --> 00:29:27.900
which is the high-level
Apigee unit of tenancy.

00:29:27.900 --> 00:29:29.920
You can think of
it as your company.

00:29:29.920 --> 00:29:32.400
And you can spin that up
in a matter of seconds.

00:29:32.400 --> 00:29:35.880
And you can then go ahead
and create API proxies

00:29:35.880 --> 00:29:38.670
and start proxying your
own services or third party

00:29:38.670 --> 00:29:41.500
APIs in no time.

00:29:41.500 --> 00:29:43.590
We have hands-on
enablement as well.

00:29:43.590 --> 00:29:46.700
There is a virtual API jam.

00:29:46.700 --> 00:29:49.120
It's a video
instruction course where

00:29:49.120 --> 00:29:50.980
you'll have an
instructor lead you

00:29:50.980 --> 00:29:54.610
through a series of
labs and workshops

00:29:54.610 --> 00:29:57.550
that take you through the
end-to-end journey of an API

00:29:57.550 --> 00:29:58.810
provider.

00:29:58.810 --> 00:30:03.340
And we also offer
on-site API jams, where

00:30:03.340 --> 00:30:05.290
we do a similar thing
but we come to you

00:30:05.290 --> 00:30:08.810
or invite you to one of
our offices with your team,

00:30:08.810 --> 00:30:12.920
and then product demo webinars
like this one and others.

00:30:12.920 --> 00:30:14.420
We've got a lot of
features covered.

00:30:14.420 --> 00:30:16.753
And we're going to continue
to cover more features based

00:30:16.753 --> 00:30:20.045
on the feedback we get from you
all to these types of sessions.

00:30:25.300 --> 00:30:28.430
So stay tuned for live Q&amp;A.
We'll be back in less than a

00:30:28.430 --> 00:30:28.930
minute.

00:34:18.270 --> 00:34:19.020
Welcome back.

00:34:19.020 --> 00:34:25.290
So I wanted to do a quick recap
before I jump into the Q&amp;A.

00:34:25.290 --> 00:34:27.870
We covered Apigee as a platform.

00:34:27.870 --> 00:34:31.230
We talked about a lot of
the API security threats

00:34:31.230 --> 00:34:35.210
that we see every day when
we're dealing with customers.

00:34:35.210 --> 00:34:41.760
And this involves standard API
security policy-type things,

00:34:41.760 --> 00:34:47.580
such as enforcing OAuth,
enforcing your AAA type

00:34:47.580 --> 00:34:50.699
authentication, as well
as traffic management,

00:34:50.699 --> 00:34:51.900
quota management.

00:34:51.900 --> 00:34:55.469
We talked about
bots, both good bots

00:34:55.469 --> 00:35:00.540
from trusted partners as
well as malicious bots,

00:35:00.540 --> 00:35:03.810
and then what features
in Apigee Edge

00:35:03.810 --> 00:35:06.180
allow you to address
those different concerns.

00:35:06.180 --> 00:35:08.010
And then we drilled
down a little bit

00:35:08.010 --> 00:35:12.120
into Apigee Sense, which is
our bot detection feature.

00:35:12.120 --> 00:35:14.460
And I gave a quick demo
of Apigee Sense that

00:35:14.460 --> 00:35:19.380
shows how you can detect bots
flowing through your APIs

00:35:19.380 --> 00:35:21.720
and how you can act on
those, either blocking

00:35:21.720 --> 00:35:25.000
traffic, allowing traffic,
or flagging traffic

00:35:25.000 --> 00:35:30.350
for special handlings, like
redirecting to a honeypot.

00:35:30.350 --> 00:35:32.930
So now, I want to jump
into some questions

00:35:32.930 --> 00:35:37.310
that we've gotten throughout the
session and that are coming in.

00:35:37.310 --> 00:35:41.950
First, what is the performance
impact of Sense on API traffic?

00:35:41.950 --> 00:35:46.120
So I touched on this briefly.

00:35:46.120 --> 00:35:50.300
I mentioned that Sense adds
less than 10 milliseconds.

00:35:50.300 --> 00:35:53.900
And essentially, you can
think of this as no impact

00:35:53.900 --> 00:35:57.880
because the detection
is all done offline.

00:35:57.880 --> 00:35:59.620
And there's zero impact.

00:35:59.620 --> 00:36:01.480
The less than 10
milliseconds is simply

00:36:01.480 --> 00:36:05.110
the time it takes us to
block or flag a call,

00:36:05.110 --> 00:36:08.170
which you would be
performing anyway

00:36:08.170 --> 00:36:12.070
through an API policy or any
type of enforcement measure.

00:36:12.070 --> 00:36:15.420
So it should feel
like no latency

00:36:15.420 --> 00:36:18.940
is added at all to proxies
when you're using Sense,

00:36:18.940 --> 00:36:21.720
to your APIs when using Sense.

00:36:21.720 --> 00:36:25.320
Next question-- is Sense
available for private cloud?

00:36:25.320 --> 00:36:28.230
And private cloud is Apigee's
term for on-premises.

00:36:28.230 --> 00:36:30.150
We didn't talk about
deployment options.

00:36:30.150 --> 00:36:33.810
But Apigee can be deployed
on the cloud where

00:36:33.810 --> 00:36:38.010
it's fully-hosted SaaS
product or on-prem.

00:36:38.010 --> 00:36:40.620
Or we have hybrid
options as well

00:36:40.620 --> 00:36:42.510
and several other derivatives.

00:36:42.510 --> 00:36:46.800
But Sense is currently
only for cloud customers.

00:36:46.800 --> 00:36:51.000
So on-prem customers are
not able to leverage Sense.

00:36:51.000 --> 00:36:53.400
And this has a lot to do
with the technologies we

00:36:53.400 --> 00:36:57.600
use to power Sense
in GCP, as well

00:36:57.600 --> 00:37:01.590
as the performance required
and the infrastructure

00:37:01.590 --> 00:37:05.970
required in order to
run this kind of system.

00:37:05.970 --> 00:37:10.250
So next was asked, is
Sense a separate product

00:37:10.250 --> 00:37:13.160
or is it included
with Apigee Edge?

00:37:13.160 --> 00:37:18.830
So Sense is a feature
within Apigee Edge.

00:37:18.830 --> 00:37:23.900
You can talk about licensing
with your account team.

00:37:23.900 --> 00:37:27.020
But it's a feature that's
part of the platform,

00:37:27.020 --> 00:37:30.620
and you can leverage it as long
as you're a cloud customer.

00:37:30.620 --> 00:37:33.230
And it is restricted to
enterprise customers.

00:37:33.230 --> 00:37:38.240
We have different pricing tiers.

00:37:38.240 --> 00:37:44.450
So our basic, which are
targeting lower-volume API

00:37:44.450 --> 00:37:46.970
programs, are not able
to leverage Sense.

00:37:46.970 --> 00:37:50.810
But all enterprise Cloud
customers have the ability

00:37:50.810 --> 00:37:52.050
to leverage Apigee Sense.

00:37:54.890 --> 00:37:57.830
Can Sense be added
at the proxy level?

00:37:57.830 --> 00:38:01.020
And can you choose which
APIs flow through Sense.

00:38:01.020 --> 00:38:03.890
So yes.

00:38:03.890 --> 00:38:09.080
You can configure the detection
rules to be API proxy specific.

00:38:09.080 --> 00:38:13.380
So if you only want to detect
on your OAuth endpoint,

00:38:13.380 --> 00:38:15.680
you can configure
rules to do that.

00:38:15.680 --> 00:38:19.470
And then protection happens
at the organization level.

00:38:23.140 --> 00:38:25.860
Finally, does Sense
look across all customer

00:38:25.860 --> 00:38:28.230
data to identify
malicious patterns?

00:38:28.230 --> 00:38:33.030
So Apigee Sense-- when
we were building Sense

00:38:33.030 --> 00:38:36.870
and as we continue to make
Sense better and smarter,

00:38:36.870 --> 00:38:42.090
we leverage the breadth of
data that we have across all

00:38:42.090 --> 00:38:43.920
of our customers on cloud.

00:38:43.920 --> 00:38:47.940
But that's solely for
building the models.

00:38:47.940 --> 00:38:51.580
That doesn't participate at all
when we're detecting traffic.

00:38:51.580 --> 00:38:57.690
So we are leveraging
our experience

00:38:57.690 --> 00:39:01.260
working with some of the most
major, some of the largest API

00:39:01.260 --> 00:39:06.150
providers in the industry
to determine and feed

00:39:06.150 --> 00:39:10.960
into the models that
allow us to surface

00:39:10.960 --> 00:39:13.590
what are the behavioral patterns
that we should be concerned

00:39:13.590 --> 00:39:14.100
with.

00:39:14.100 --> 00:39:16.740
But once those
models are defined--

00:39:16.740 --> 00:39:19.890
and we're ever
tuning those models--

00:39:19.890 --> 00:39:22.860
those models get
applied completely

00:39:22.860 --> 00:39:25.650
within a particular
Apigee organization.

00:39:25.650 --> 00:39:28.500
And I touched on it,
but just to confirm,

00:39:28.500 --> 00:39:31.530
an organization is
a tenant in Apigee.

00:39:31.530 --> 00:39:33.780
So Apigee a
multi-tenant solution.

00:39:33.780 --> 00:39:38.370
And your company would likely
be a single or possibly

00:39:38.370 --> 00:39:40.300
more than one organization.

00:39:40.300 --> 00:39:42.840
So the model is applied
to your organization.

00:39:42.840 --> 00:39:47.490
And the detection and
protection is done solely

00:39:47.490 --> 00:39:50.590
within the confines of that
organization, completely

00:39:50.590 --> 00:39:53.050
sandboxed from all other
data within Apogee.

00:39:53.050 --> 00:39:57.298
So your data is never
commingled with any other data.

00:39:59.986 --> 00:40:00.780
All right.

00:40:00.780 --> 00:40:07.770
So that wraps up the questions
that we received that were not

00:40:07.770 --> 00:40:11.320
already answered individually.

00:40:11.320 --> 00:40:13.920
So I just wanted to thank
you all for your time

00:40:13.920 --> 00:40:16.920
and remind you to stay tuned
for the next session, which

00:40:16.920 --> 00:40:18.390
is get the most
out of your audit

00:40:18.390 --> 00:40:21.780
logs with these
configuration best practices,

00:40:21.780 --> 00:40:25.140
broadcasting live from
our New York office.

